{
  "id": "COMPOUND:36621 - PROTEIN:5790",
  "verbalization": "Cholesta-8-en-3beta,5alpha,6alpha,25-tetrol is implicated in the biological activity of HPAC through a series of target relationships that converge on UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1-8. This enzyme serves as a common intermediary in the pathways of three different compounds—Butyl 2-(2,5-dihydroxyphenyl)acetate, Barringtogenol C, and Melafolone—each of which is known to target HPAC. The evidence suggests that Cholesta-8-en-3beta,5alpha,6alpha,25-tetrol may influence HPAC through the regulation of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1-8, which in turn modulates the activity of the target compounds. The overlapping targets and the consistent presence of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1-8 as a common point of interaction imply a potential mechanism by which Cholesta-8-en-3beta,5alpha,6alpha,25-tetrol could influence HPAC, likely through the modulation of metabolic pathways involving this enzyme. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1-8 in mediating the effects of these compounds on HPAC, suggesting a broader role for this enzyme in the biological response to these substances.",
